what's up guys helen here now i don't  know about some of you luddites but i  actually revel in the convenience of  modern technology still enjoying these  addictive pleasures does mean you're  bowing down at the altar of big tech and  really one company in particular google  which along with its parent company  alphabet has infiltrated just about  every aspect of our lives we use google  chrome to browse the internet android to  power our phones nest to keep our homes  cool fitbit to track our literal steps  youtube to watch our favorite content  and the list just keeps going and  surprise surprise being beholden to a  corporate overlord isn't without its  downfalls i mean this is the company  that collects a truly omniscient amount  of information about you in my case 1.97  gigabytes which begs the very simple  question is google evil to answer that  we're going to have to understand the  changing role data plays in our lives  and the dubious ways google has grown to  monetize them so join us for this  wisecrack edition on how google got evil  in order to assess just how sinister  google might be we first need to  understand how search engines work cast  your minds back to the year 1996 a  simpler time when pokemon numbered only  151  also a time when search engines really  sucked then search engines would rank  pages based on how many times a search  phrase appeared which is kind of dumb  after all if your friend's choking on a  bagel bite you don't want the first  website you click on to be the phrase  heimlich maneuver written a thousand  times enter the stanford wonderkins of  90's tech larry page and sergey brin  they radically envisioned the internet  as a literal web with different web  pages connected via links see larry  sergey i.e the benifer of the internet  wagered that the more referrals any one  page has the more important that page  must be like how on a map the more roads  that lead to a city the more important  that city probably is so larry sergey  set out to literally map the internet  via algorithm and the two were able to  create a system in which more popular  and relevant websites rose to the top of  the search results it was a massively  ambitious undertaking that would  fundamentally change the internet  forever by 1998 what started as a  doctorate thesis matured into a  massively successful company google  after one year google was already  handling 3.5 million searches a day by  2000 that number had grown to 18 million  but while google was undoubtedly the  best search engine around the company  still hadn't monetized that's because  monetizing created an ethical quandary  there was massive concern for example  that google might let companies  artificially buy their way to a higher  search ranking but larry sergey decided  this would be manipulative and harmful  to the consumer so they came up with two  big breakthroughs the first breakthrough  was adwords you know that sponsored  content at the top of your google search  results that says add this allowed  google to rake in money from companies  trying to get their products seen  without creating a perverse pay-to-play  system the second breakthrough was the  company's motto don't be evil this would  act as google's moral compass and the  company's early years encouraging them  to forego short-term gains in order to  be good for the world they've since  dropped the tagline which bold move and  for a while google did indeed seem to be  the good guys but as google embarked on  its advertising venture it soon realized  it could create tailor-made ads to suit  each customer this was a real game  changer for an advertising industry that  previously could only try to target  consumers via vague demographics that's  because google had a secret weapon all  the data from your previous searches  thus it knew a lot about you while this  data was once only used to refine search  results google quickly realized that it  offered key insights which could be used  to better serve us ads according to  harvard's psychologist shashana zubov  the collecting and monetizing of  personal data marked a big inflection  point not just for google but for  society as a whole see google had  pioneered what duboff dubs surveillance  capitalism i.e a new economic order that  claims human experience as free raw  material for hidden commercial practices  of extraction prediction and sales in  other words we as an amalgamation of our  data have become the product which  depending on your perspective is  probably explicitly evil a little bit  icky or somewhere in between especially  if you like me keep getting served  personalized ads for bagel bites on  every single website and once google saw  how profitable creeping could be they  really doubled down on the business  model in 2007 it acquired the company  doubleclick which had a simple mo  serving up personalized banner ads on  websites all over the internet  doubleclick essentially created the  current advertising hellscape that we  live in now google didn't just know  everything that you were searching for  via doubleclick it also knew which  websites you were visiting which  articles you were reading movies you  were watching close you were shopping in  2010 then google ceo eric schmidt  famously declared we know where you are  we know where you've been we can more or  less know what you're thinking about  which doesn't sound not evil but it's  really not an exaggeration almost every  product the company has is dedicated to  collecting your data from gmail scanning  your inbox to fitbit tracking your sleep  patterns to google maps tracking your  last trip to the liquor store hey it's  five o'clock somewhere all said and done  it's not that google has the capacity to  store at least 15 exabytes of data or 15  billion gigabytes and a sizable portion  of that is dedicated to storing our  personal data now some of you may be  asking what's really the harm in being  served personalized ads well when you  look more closely there's a lot of  potential problems for one google has  used its data collecting to help  companies partake in exploitative  pricing specifically that means setting  whatever price points they choose for  each individual customer based on their  data and personalization can have  particularly harmful effects on people  in dire economic straits for years  google used its data collection to help  payday loan lenders advertise to  vulnerable potential borrowers google's  even been caught giving data to the  police based on keyword searches that  makes me shiver when i recall all the  times i've googled where does the  creator of vagobytes live and best ways  to avenge your friend but even when it's  just using data to decide what  information to feed you online the  results can be low-key disastrous for  example you might think a fringe  political cause is a lot more sensible  than it really is simply because you  served a continual stream of ads and  youtube videos on the topic in fact  researchers believe that algorithms  played a key role in radicalizing people  by serving them progressively more  extreme content and it's this very  mechanism which has given oxygen to so  many of the most absurd and stupid  conspiracy theories of recent years many  of which thrived on google's own youtube  and how did that turn out in reality  you are actually in a giant  planetarium slash terrarium  slash soundstage and sure even if you're  completely on team personalized content  that doesn't really clear google of all  evil related charges the fundamental  problem remains when a company is  sitting on so much of your personal  information there is ample opportunity  for abuse in countries where google  operates it's forced to play ball with  local authorities in rare instances  google has even been forced to turn over  user account information to less than  stellar governments like saudi arabia  and while plans have since been  shuttered google was even developing a  special search engine to be compliant  with china's rigorous state censorship  but google doesn't have to be  collaborating with oppressive  governments to potentially influence  your life and your choices as zuboff  explained surveillance capitalists have  learned to tune herd and condition our  behavior with subtle and subliminal cues  rewards and punishments that shunt us  toward their most profitable outcomes in  this way zuboff notes our behavior is  being intervened upon touched coaxed  modified contaged influenced in all of  these ways that are designed to be  undetectable to us zubalf argues that  this behavioral modification born out of  excessive data collection and the  personalization it facilitates even robs  us of our future tense which is the  essence of free will in other words do i  even like bagel bites anymore or am i  merely a carb hungry sheep being  controlled by the man but why are we so  okay with google owning the sum of our  life experiences why are we cool with a  business model that postures us free  will simultaneously making us pay by  handing over our valuable data well on  one level it's because we're not totally  sure what exactly is happening which is  also true of me whenever i sit behind  this microphone as zuboff explains  google has learned to employ a range of  rhetorical political and technological  strategies to obfuscate these processes  and their implications effectively it  hides its shady practices from consumers  while heavily lobbying the government  for the right to continue them but  there's more according to bruce schneier  our continued reliance on google despite  its creepiness comes down to the fact  that we overestimate the value of free  we consume more of something than we  should when it's free we pressure others  to consume it free warps our normal  sense of cost versus benefit and people  end up trading their personal data for  less than it's worth on the surface it  seems like we're getting a great deal  with google but the truth of that matter  is that we're handing the company some  of our most sensitive information  information that's tremendously valuable  to companies trying to advertise to us  indeed personal data is the fastest  growing industry in america based on  current trends our data could be worth  197.7 billion dollars by 2022 making it  bigger than the nation's entire  agricultural output but as valuable as  it is we're of course not compensated  for it as journalist rana foruhar argues  when our data is what's being collected  we have a right to know how much it's  worth and then decide as a society if we  should be receiving some of that value  ourselves and when you think about how  many bagel bites your data could buy you  that argument becomes pretty persuasive  so is google kind of evil for sure  whether they're using reams of data to  manipulate your behavior via  hyper-targeted ads making conspiracy  theories seem mainstream or colluding  with bad actors like digital loan sharks  or oppressive governments google's power  is overwhelming to contemplate meanwhile  their willingness to use that power for  good is underwhelming but what do you  guys think are we being too harsh on the  very company which lets us publish these  videos to reach all our lovely fans or  are we hitting on a fundamental truth  that for too long we've been willing to  sacrifice privacy for convenience let us  know what you think in the comments big  thanks to our patrons for all your  support and be sure to check out our  podcast obliterate that subscribe button  like google did to your privacy and  don't forget to ring that bell and as  always thanks for watching peace  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]  you
 

